WEBVTT

1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,711
A practical cloud IAM workflow starts with inventory.

2
00:00:03,711 --> 00:00:16,458
List human users, groups, roles, service accounts, managed identities, workload identities, applications, API keys, tokens, temporary credential patterns, and emergency accounts.

3
00:00:16,458 --> 00:00:26,059
Assign owners and classify whether the access is human, workload, automation, administrative, production, development, or third-party.

4
00:00:26,059 --> 00:00:31,061
If an identity has no owner or purpose, it is already a governance problem.

5
00:00:31,061 --> 00:00:34,208
Next, define tasks and grant least privilege.

6
00:00:34,208 --> 00:00:47,197
Start from what the person or workload needs to do, map required actions to resources, scope permissions narrowly, use conditions where appropriate, and separate duties for sensitive work.

7
00:00:47,197 --> 00:00:56,152
Avoid broad administrator, owner, contributor, wildcard, and full-access permissions unless there is a clear, approved reason.

8
00:00:56,152 --> 00:01:00,832
Review effective access, not only the most visible role assignment.

9
00:01:00,832 --> 00:01:04,624
Use federation and MFA for human access where possible.

10
00:01:04,624 --> 00:01:13,418
Prefer temporary credentials, role assumption, just-in-time access, and managed identity patterns over long-lived secrets.

11
00:01:13,418 --> 00:01:21,808
Protect workload identities with narrow deployment roles, environment separation, secret rotation, and auditability.

12
00:01:21,808 --> 00:01:30,683
Treat CI/CD pipelines, containers, virtual machines, serverless functions, and service accounts as first-class IAM subjects.

13
00:01:30,683 --> 00:01:33,668
Finally, monitor and review continuously.

14
00:01:33,668 --> 00:01:36,169
Log IAM activity and cloud API usage.

15
00:01:36,169 --> 00:01:48,432
Watch for policy changes, key creation, trust relationship changes, role passing, impersonation, denied access, unusual API calls, and changes to logging or security controls.

16
00:01:48,432 --> 00:01:56,823
Review access regularly, remove stale identities, track exceptions, protect emergency accounts, and measure progress.

17
00:01:56,823 --> 00:02:04,729
Secure cloud IAM depends on identity lifecycle, permission design, monitoring, and governance working together.

18
00:02:04,729 --> 00:02:09,086
A compact review checklist helps keep the practice repeatable.

19
00:02:09,086 --> 00:02:11,345
Can every identity be explained?

20
00:02:11,345 --> 00:02:14,169
Does every privileged role have an owner?

21
00:02:14,169 --> 00:02:17,073
Are human users federated where possible?

22
00:02:17,073 --> 00:02:21,430
Are workloads using short-lived or managed identity patterns?

23
00:02:21,430 --> 00:02:24,576
Are long-lived keys justified and monitored?

24
00:02:24,576 --> 00:02:27,803
Are sensitive IAM changes logged and reviewed?

25
00:02:27,803 --> 00:02:30,143
Are stale grants removed quickly?

26
00:02:30,143 --> 00:02:33,693
The final lesson is that cloud IAM is never finished.

27
00:02:33,693 --> 00:02:39,260
New projects, pipelines, vendors, identities, and services appear constantly.

28
00:02:39,260 --> 00:02:43,132
Secure programs build review into the operating rhythm.

29
00:02:43,132 --> 00:02:49,425
They make safe access easy to request, risky access visible, and stale access easy to remove.

30
00:02:49,425 --> 00:02:56,202
That is how IAM becomes a durable cloud security foundation instead of a growing list of exceptions.
